This suite runs the pynfs NFSv4.0, 4.1 and 4.2 server conformance tests
against the ganesha (FSAL_CEPH) export, one job per version. pynfs uses its
own NFS client, so there is no kernel mount here.

Delegations are enabled (cephadm's ganesha config disables them) so that the
DELEG tests exercise libcephfs delegations.

pynfs always exits 0. The pynfs task parses its JSON results and fails the
job on any failed test that is not listed under known_failures in
2-pynfs.yaml. The known failures, and why they fail:

  COMP3 LINK8 LOOK7 OPEN13 RM5 RNM8 RNM9 SEC6   (4.0; COMP3 RNM8 RNM9 on 4.1/4.2)
    These tests send path components, or a COMPOUND tag (COMP3), that are not
    valid UTF-8 and expect NFS4ERR_INVAL. Ganesha treats them as plain bytes
    unless Enforce_UTF8_Validation is set in its NFSv4 config block, which
    is off by default and which mgr/nfs user config cannot set here. This is
    not a ganesha bug: for NFSv4.0, RFC 7530 says the server MAY return
    NFS4ERR_INVAL and allows servers that accept such names. For NFSv4.1 and
    later, RFC 8881 says that the server "should" return it.

  WRT16 WRT17   (4.0)
    pynfs bug: float division under python3 in st_write.py makes the tests
    error out before checking the server.

  PUTFH3   (4.0)
    A filehandle of a removed file is still usable (NFS4_OK, not
    NFS4ERR_STALE). The test notes that a server is allowed to do this.

  ALLOC1 ALLOC2 ALLOC3   (4.2)
    ALLOCATE returns NFS4ERR_NOTSUPP. Ganesha (FSAL_CEPH) implements it as
    ceph_ll_fallocate() with mode 0, which libcephfs rejects with EOPNOTSUPP
    as it does not support plain preallocation. This is not a ganesha bug,
    and it is allowed by the protocol: ALLOCATE is OPTIONAL in NFSv4.2
    (RFC 7862). The pynfs tests do not accept NFS4ERR_NOTSUPP and fail.

When a ganesha, libcephfs or pynfs fix lands, drop the test from
known_failures. The task logs any known failure that did not fail.

These DELEG tests are deliberately not known failures, so the NFSv4.1 and 4.2
jobs fail until ganesha is fixed:

  DELEG7
    Ganesha does not implement BACKCHANNEL_CTL, which RFC 8881 makes REQUIRED:
    it returns NFS4ERR_OP_ILLEGAL and keeps sending CB_RECALL with the
    credentials given at CREATE_SESSION.

  DELEG24 DELEG25
    These test CB_GETATTR on a write delegation and need the
    FATTR4_OPEN_ARGUMENTS attribute. The cause of the failure is not yet
    established.
